When a timer callback’s microtask checkpoint schedules a timer,
Ladybird gives that timer the callback’s nesting level plus one, so
the 4ms clamp applies past level 5. Blink does the same: DOMTimer::
Fired() sets DOMTimerCoordinator’s timer nesting level for as long as
action->Execute() runs, and the checkpoint runs inside it. Gecko keeps
the level in TimeoutManager::SetNestingLevelForWindow() for the whole
of handler->Call() in nsGlobalWindowInner::RunTimeoutHandler(). And
WebKit’s DOMTimerFireState scope sets the context’s nesting level
around m_action in DOMTimer::fired(). The HTML spec’s text makes each
microtask the currently running task, so a timer scheduled from one
would start over at level 0. None of the three engines does that: In
Chrome, a 30-hop chain of such timers takes 242ms.
